sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Differences of electric potential between cell constituents, and between parts of the egg, play a great part in cell division; the potential difference between animal and vegetative poles of a fertilised egg fluctuates or reverses at successive segmentations, and the electrical difference of chromatin is greatest at mitosis.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Differences of electric potential play a great part in cell division";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Electrical potential in cell division”. Scope: dividing cells and fertilised eggs (e.g. Fundulus, toad, turtle)"
}
